.TH IPGEN 1 "2026-09-30" "ipgen 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ipgen \- algorithmic IPv4 range generator with pause and resume
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ipgen
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B ipgen generate
.RB [ --preset
.IR PRESET ]
.RB [ --categories
.IR CAT ,...]
.RB [ --include
.IR SPEC ]...
.RB [ --exclude
.IR SPEC ]...
.RB [ --order
.IR ORDER ]
.RB [ --seed
.IR N ]
.RB [ --prefix-len
.IR N ]
.RB [ --shard
.IR K/M ]
.RB [ --count
.IR N ]
.RB [ --batch
.IR N ]
.RB [ --format
.IR FORMAT ]
.RB [ --out
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ --checkpoint-every
.IR N ]
.RB [ --state
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B ipgen resume
.RB [ --state
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ --batch
.IR N ]
.RB [ --count
.IR N ]
.RB [ --format
.IR FORMAT ]
.RB [ --out
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ --checkpoint-every
.IR N ]
.br
.B ipgen status
.RB [ --state
.IR FILE ]
.br
.B ipgen categories
.RB [ --detailed ]
.br
.B ipgen ranges
.RB [ --preset
.IR PRESET ]
.RB [ --categories
.IR CAT ,...]
.RB [ --include
.IR SPEC ]...
.RB [ --exclude
.IR SPEC ]...
.RB [ --summary ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ipgen
is a small, fast, dependency-light Rust tool that algorithmically
enumerates IPv4 addresses, CIDRs, and aligned
.I /prefix
blocks. There are no pre-generated lists on disk and no databases;
the next address is computed on demand from a tiny piece of state
.RI ( (config,\ cursor) )
that fits in a JSON file. That state is enough to stop a run at any
moment and continue later \(em in another process, on another machine,
after a crash, or after a reboot \(em with no duplicates and no gaps.

.PP
The tool is organised as a Cargo workspace with three crates:
.TP
.B ipgen-core
the library: all generator logic, no networking code.
.TP
.B ipgen-cli
the
.B ipgen
binary that most users will run.
.TP
.B ipgen-mcp
the
.B ipgen-mcp
binary, a stdio Model Context Protocol (MCP) server for AI agents.

.PP
.B STRICT NON-GOAL:
.B ipgen
only enumerates addresses. It contains no sockets, no probing, no
port scanning, no DNS. Use the output only against networks you own
or are explicitly authorised to examine.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR generate " (default)"
Generate a stream of addresses or CIDRs. Honours the selection
arguments (\fB--preset\fR, \fB--categories\fR, \fB--include\fR,
\fB--exclude\fR), the ordering arguments (\fB--order\fR, \fB--seed\fR,
\fB--prefix-len\fR), the sharding argument (\fB--shard\fR), and the
output arguments (\fB--count\fR, \fB--batch\fR, \fB--format\fR,
\fB--out\fR, \fB--checkpoint-every\fR, \fB--state\fR).
.TP
.B resume
Resume generation from a checkpoint file written by a previous run or
by an interrupted
.B generate
call. Honours \fB--state\fR, \fB--batch\fR, \fB--count\fR,
\fB--format\fR, \fB--out\fR, \fB--checkpoint-every\fR.
.TP
.B status
Print session state (session id, config hash, order, shard, cursor,
total, percent, remaining, timestamps) without generating. Honours
\fB--state\fR.
.TP
.B categories
List the 13 built-in IANA special-purpose categories with their sizes
and percentages of the full \(2^32\) space. With \fB--detailed\fR, also
print the actual CIDRs per category.
.TP
.B ranges
Print the maximal aligned CIDR blocks covering the selected address
set. With \fB--summary\fR, print a compact JSON summary
.RI ( {"cidr_count":N,"total_addresses":M} )
instead of the full CIDR list.

.SH SELECTION OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --preset = PRESET
Shorthand for a category list. One of:
.B public
(default),
.B private,
.B all,
.BR "public+private" .
.TP
.BR --categories = CAT1,CAT2,...
Explicit comma-separated list of category names, overriding
\fB--preset\fR. Valid names (case-insensitive, hyphens or underscores
interchangeable):
.BR this-network ,
.BR private ,
.BR shared-cgnat ,
.BR loopback ,
.BR link-local ,
.BR ietf-protocol ,
.BR documentation
.RI ( or docs ),
.BR deprecated-6to4
.RI ( or 6to4 ),
.BR benchmarking ,
.BR multicast ,
.BR reserved ,
.BR broadcast ,
.BR public .
.TP
.BR --include = SPEC
Extra included CIDR, range
.RI ( a.b.c.d-e.f.g.h ),
or single address. Repeatable. Specs are unioned with the preset /
categories selection.
.TP
.BR --exclude = SPEC
Extra excluded CIDR, range, or single address. Repeatable. Specs are
subtracted from the selection after includes are applied.

.SH ORDERING OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --order = ORDER
How to map the cursor to an output position. One of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
.B sequential
\(em ascending order over the allowed set. Fastest path; bulk-fills
the output buffer interval by interval with no per-address binary
search. Use when you do not need spread.
.IP \(bu 3
.B permuted
(default) \(em keyed pseudo-random bijection over
.I [0,\ N)
implemented as a cycle-walking balanced Feistel network. Not
cryptographic; it is a cheap deterministic spread suitable for survey
workloads. Use this when you want addresses spread across the whole
internet rather than clustered in one subnet.
.IP \(bu 3
.B blocks
\(em iterate aligned
.I /prefix-len
blocks (default
.IR /24 )
in permuted order. Each emitted line is a CIDR plus (in
.B jsonl
or
.B csv
formats) the count of allowed addresses that fall inside the block.
Use for block-level mapping or for feeding into
.BR nmap\ -iL .
.RE
.TP
.BR --seed = N
Seed for the
.B permuted
and
.B blocks
orderings. Same
.RI ( seed,\ config )
always yields the same sequence (reproducible runs). Default
.BR 0 .
.TP
.BR --prefix-len = N
Block prefix length for
.B blocks
order (8..=32, default 24). Ignored for other orders.

.SH SHARDING
.TP
.BR --shard = K/M
Run as worker
.I K
of
.I M
total. Emit indices
.I i
where
.IR "i % M == K" .
Shards are pairwise disjoint and together cover the full stream
exactly once. Each shard needs its own
.B --state
and
.B --out
files. Default
.BR 0/1 .

.SH OUTPUT OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --count = N
Emit at most
.I N
items. Default: emit until the stream is exhausted or
.B Ctrl-C
is received.
.TP
.BR --batch = N
Batch size per internal call (default 4096, max 1048576). Larger
batches are more efficient but use more memory. For high-throughput
runs to a file, use 65536 or larger.
.TP
.BR --format = FORMAT
Output format. One of:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
.B text
(default) \(em one address (or CIDR) per line.
.IP \(bu 3
.B jsonl
\(em one JSON object per line:
.RI ( {"index":I,"address":"A","category":"C"} )
for addresses,
.RI ( {"index":I,"cidr":"C","count":N} )
for blocks.
.IP \(bu 3
.B csv
\(em three columns
.RI ( index,address,category )
or
.RI ( index,cidr,count )
with a header row.
.RE
.TP
.BR --out = FILE
Write to
.I FILE
instead of stdout. The file is opened fresh on
.B generate
and appended to on
.B resume
\(em use the same
.B --out
across pause/resume to keep the output contiguous.
.TP
.BR --checkpoint-every = N
Write a checkpoint every
.I N
batches (0 = only at exit). With the default batch size of 4096,
.IR N =1000
writes a checkpoint roughly every 4 million addresses.
.TP
.BR --state = FILE
Path for the checkpoint file (default
.IR ipgen.state.json ).
The checkpoint stores
.RI ( config,\ cursor )
plus a content hash; tampering with the config without recomputing
the hash is refused on load.

.SH EXIT CODES
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (bad flag, unknown category, empty selection, etc.).
.TP
.B 130
.B Ctrl-C
(or
.BR SIGINT )
\(em after the checkpoint has been flushed.
.TP
.B 3
Output pipe closed (e.g. when piped into
.BR "head\ -n\ N" );
the generator exits cleanly.

.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGINT
(Ctrl-C)
Flush the current checkpoint and exit with code 130. The state file
is written atomically; resume with
.B ipgen\ resume
to continue from the saved cursor.

.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Print the 13 built-in categories and their sizes:
.B ipgen categories
.TP
Print the categories with the actual CIDRs per category:
.B ipgen categories --detailed
.TP
Generate the first 5 public IPv4 addresses in ascending order:
.B ipgen generate --preset public --order sequential --count 5
.TP
Generate 10 public addresses in permuted order with a fixed seed:
.B ipgen generate --preset public --order permuted --seed 42 --count 10
.TP
Generate all public IPv4 addresses in order (3.7 billion addresses):
.B ipgen generate --preset public --order sequential --batch 65536 --checkpoint-every 1000 --state public-run.state.json --out public-ipv4.txt
.TP
Generate the private (RFC 1918) addresses:
.B ipgen generate --preset private --order sequential
.TP
Generate CIDR blocks instead of addresses:
.B ipgen generate --preset public --order blocks --prefix-len 24 --count 5
.TP
Generate with custom includes and excludes:
.B ipgen generate --preset public --include 9.9.9.0/24 --exclude 9.9.9.128/25 --order sequential
.TP
Start a long run, pause with Ctrl-C, inspect, resume:
.nf
.RS
ipgen generate --preset public --order permuted --seed 7 --out addrs.txt --checkpoint-every 100
# Ctrl-C
ipgen status --state ipgen.state.json
ipgen resume --state ipgen.state.json --out addrs.txt
.RE
.fi
.TP
Run 4 shards in parallel on one machine:
.nf
.RS
for k in 0 1 2 3; do
  ipgen generate --preset public --order permuted --seed 7 \\
    --shard $k/4 --state shard-$k.state.json --out shard-$k.txt &
done
wait
.RE
.fi
.TP
Print the merged CIDR list for the private set:
.B ipgen ranges --preset private
.TP
Print a compact JSON summary for the public set:
.B ipgen ranges --preset public --summary
.PD
.RE

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B IPGEN_BIN
Used by the
.IR skills/ipgen/scripts/ipgen.sh
wrapper to locate the
.B ipgen
binary. Resolution order:
.BR $IPGEN_BIN ,
.IR ./dist/ipgen ,
.IR ./bin/<platform>/ipgen ,
.IR ./target/release/ipgen ,
then
.B PATH
lookup.
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Respected by
.B ipgen-mcp
for log filtering. Default
.BR off .

.SH FILES
.TP
.B ipgen.state.json
Default checkpoint file. Stores
.RI ( config,\ cursor )
plus a content hash. Written atomically via temp file + fsync + rename.
A crash at any point leaves either the previous checkpoint intact or a
temp file alongside it; the loader never sees a half-written file.

